\section{Unification Requires Shared Questions, Not One Semantics}

Array trains, stream actors, spreadsheet cells, stack words, rewrite rules, and spatial kernels all expose dependencies, yet they disagree about readiness, state, ordering, rates, and failure. A unified alternative-language IR should therefore share infrastructure while retaining dialect-specific contracts. MLIR demonstrates the value of extensible operations, regions, types, and attributes across abstraction levels \cite{lattner2021mlir}; it does not imply that every source language should be flattened immediately into one universal opcode set.

One possible textual form keeps semantic traits visible:

\begin{verbatim}
%y = alt.node "matmul"(%x, %w)
     {shape = ["N","K","M"], purity = "pure"}
     : (!tensor, !tensor) -> !tensor
alt.channel %y -> %sink
     {order = "fifo", produce = 1, consume = 1}
\end{verbatim}

The common layer provides stable IDs, typed ports, graph edges, nested regions, symbolic attributes, effects, locations, and verification hooks. Dialects define what firing, evaluation, receive, rewrite, or spatial update means. ``Has incoming edges'' is not a sufficient common execution rule.

\section{Build a Semantic Spine with Explicit Extensions}

The spine should distinguish immutable values from token streams, state cells from buffers, control regions from graph topology, and effects from ordinary data. Edges name port positions and transport contracts. Operations may own regions for an actor action, spreadsheet formula, stack block, or transition rule. Interfaces expose shape inference, rate inference, purity, scheduling constraints, and provenance without requiring every operation to implement every interface.

