Output artifacts should include serialized IR at selected stages, verifier reports, schedule or demand plans, target code, runtime descriptors, and a provenance map. Versioned schemas and dialect names are part of interoperability: an unknown attribute that affects semantics must not be silently dropped by an older reader.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Lower Only When the Required Interface Is Proven}

\begin{verbatim}
parse source into its semantics-preserving dialect
run dialect verifier and attach stable operation and edge IDs
for candidate conversion:
    query required interfaces: rates, effects, shapes, state, ordering
    if proofs and residual guards cover every requirement:
        rewrite to lower dialect and record proof obligations discharged
    else:
        retain operation or lower through its runtime fallback
verify the mixed-dialect module after every conversion batch
\end{verbatim}

Legal common optimizations include canonicalizing pure value subgraphs, deleting unreachable regions, and composing verified adapters. Fusion or scheduling is legal only when relevant dialect interfaces establish compatible token, effect, state, and error behavior. A graph rewrite proved for fixed-rate FIFO actors cannot be applied to a selective mailbox or volatile spreadsheet cell because their shapes look alike.

Preservation is validated at each dialect conversion using structural verifiers, translation validation, differential interpreters, and retained source IDs. The final test suite compares values, traces, errors, blocking, and external effects appropriate to each source contract. This layered approach yields reusable compiler machinery without erasing the distinctions that make the source languages meaningful.

\section{Distinguish an Unknown Fact From a Proven Unit Rate}

Suppose an extension declares a producer that sometimes emits zero tokens and sometimes emits one, depending on a guard. A scheduling interface that returns the integer \(1\) when no static rate is known will accidentally declare this actor unit-rate. Static scheduling needs an affirmative proof. Rate inference can instead return a known constant, a known interval, or an unknown fact; guard analysis may refine the interval at a particular use.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Rate fact & What is known & Unit-rate conversion \\
\hline
Exactly one & Every firing produces one & May proceed \\
Zero or one & Depends on the action & Needs refinement \\
Unknown & No valid bound supplied & Refuse by default \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
require_unit_rate(op, proof_environment):
    fact := query_port_rate(op, proof_environment)
    match fact:
        Exact(1, proof): return Verified(proof)
        Exact(other, proof): return Unsupported(other)
        Interval(bounds): return NeedProof(bounds)
        Unknown: return NeedRateInterface
\end{verbatim}

Even an exact fact does not establish that the surrounding graph has an admissible periodic schedule; it is just a scheduling input. Store the scope and assumptions of the proof, including which action paths it covers. If a later transformation alters the guard or introduces a new action, invalidate that evidence and query again. A useful interoperability test supplies one verified pass-through actor and one conditional producer through the same dialect boundary. Let the conditional actor emit nothing in its first tested firing; a default-to-one backend either overconsumes a queue or stalls looking for a token that the source never promises.

\paragraph{Preserve operations that cannot yet be lowered.}
If a target supports both periodic and event-driven execution, keep
the conditional producer in a residual dynamic region. Do not just
label its output periodic: require an explicit adapter whose queue
and visibility policy supports the connection. If there is no such
adapter, raise a diagnostic at the edge, with the producer location and
the consumer's rate requirement. Unsupported lowering and malformed
source IR should remain distinguishable errors.
